+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+The strongest legal-native repositories in this list are `LexNLP`, `eyecite`, `CourtListener`, `ContraxSuite`, and `Opennyai`. For broader legal workflows, the best open source stack usually combines a PDF/OCR tool such as `Marker` or `OCRmyPDF`, a local model runner such as `Ollama`, and a private workspace such as `Open WebUI` or `AnythingLLM`.
+
+### Are there any truly open source legal AI tools with GitHub repos?
+Yes. This list only includes projects with public GitHub repositories. That is the main filter. If a tool had no GitHub repo or depended on a closed proprietary-only product, it was excluded.
+
+### What is the best AI tool for contract review if I want self-hosting?
+`LexPredict/lexpredict-contraxsuite` is the most direct contract-review platform in the list. In practice, many teams will also pair `lexpredict-lexnlp` with `Unstructured`, `Marker`, or `Open WebUI` to build a narrower private workflow around their own contracts.
+
+### Which open source AI tools help lawyers process scanned PDFs?
+`OCRmyPDF`, `Marker`, `Surya`, `Layout Parser`, `PDFText`, and `pypdf` are the most relevant picks here. `OCRmyPDF` is excellent for searchable scans, while `Marker` and `Surya` are stronger when layout, OCR quality, or structured export matters.
+
+### What is the best private ChatGPT-style tool for lawyers with local documents?
+`Open WebUI` and `AnythingLLM` are the most practical end-user interfaces in this list. `Ollama` is the best local model runner to pair with them, and `Haystack`, `LlamaIndex`, or `LangChain` become relevant when you need a more custom legal retrieval system.
+
+### Are there enough open source GitHub repos for lawyer-specific AI?
+Not really. Legal-native open source AI remains a smaller niche than general RAG, OCR, and local LLM tooling. That is why this repository includes adjacent infrastructure that lawyers can actually use, and why the audio category is explicitly documented as a gap.
+
