+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for helping improve `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must include a public GitHub repository URL.
+- Repositories without a `github.com` URL will be rejected.
+- Proprietary SaaS products, closed-source tools, and “open core” marketing pages without a public repo will be rejected.
+- The tool must be relevant to lawyers, legal ops, compliance, legal research, contract review, document extraction, private RAG, or legal-audio workflows.
+- Include the following with every suggestion:
+  - Repository name
+  - GitHub URL
+  - Short explanation of why it is useful for legal workflows
+  - Current stars count
+  - Primary language
+  - License
+  - Last commit date
+
+## Preferred Format
+
+Use this template in your issue or pull request:
+
+```md
+### Tool Name
+- GitHub: https://github.com/owner/repo
+- Why it belongs: ...
+- Stars: ...
+- Language: ...
+- License: ...
+- Last Commit: ...
+- Category: ...
+- Best for: ...
+```
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer verified, maintained repositories over speculative projects.
+- Do not submit duplicate tools.
+- If a tool is only tangentially useful for lawyers, explain the legal use case clearly.
+- If a category has few good repos, we prefer documenting the gap over adding weak entries.
+
+## Pull Requests
+
+- Keep README formatting consistent.
+- Preserve the existing heading structure.
+- Update metadata when replacing stale entries.
+- Do not remove working links unless the repository is gone, archived beyond usefulness, or no longer public.
+
+## License
+
+By contributing, you agree that your contributions will be licensed under the MIT License in this repository.
