+# Best AI Tools for Lawyers in 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026?style=social)
+
+> This curated list of the best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 focuses on open source projects that legal teams can inspect, self-host, adapt, and extend. Every entry below points to a public GitHub repository, and the list deliberately favors practical tools for legal research, contract review, PDF extraction, private document Q&A, and transcript-heavy workflows over generic hype.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp` is still one of the clearest legal-native NLP libraries for clause, amount, citation, and legal text extraction.
+- `freelawproject/eyecite` and `freelawproject/courtlistener` are the strongest open source legal research infrastructure picks in this list.
+- `datalab-to/marker`, `Unstructured-IO/unstructured`, and `ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF` are excellent for turning messy PDFs into searchable, structured inputs for downstream AI.
+- `open-webui/open-webui`, `Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm`, `langgenius/dify`, and `ollama/ollama` are the most practical self-hosted stacks for private legal knowledge assistants.
+- Voice tooling is thinner than document tooling; `SYSTRAN/faster-whisper`, `m-bain/whisperX`, `pyannote/pyannote-audio`, and `ggml-org/whisper.cpp` are the strongest verified repos for deposition, call, and interview transcription workflows.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source AI matters for legal work because lawyers handle privileged documents, regulated records, sensitive negotiations, and audit-heavy review processes. The best AI tools for lawyers in 2026 are not just “smart”; they also make it possible to inspect code, self-host inference, control retention, and plug into existing legal document pipelines without forcing a closed SaaS dependency.
+
+This list is intentionally strict. Every tool below has a public GitHub repository, and categories with thin coverage are called out as gaps instead of being padded with weak or proprietary options.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Legal Research, Citation, and Legal NLP
+
+#### [LexNLP](https://github.com/LexPredict/lexpredict-lexnlp)
+> **Description:** LexNLP’s README describes it as a library for working with real, unstructured legal text across contracts, plans, policies, procedures, and similar materials. It highlights legal-aware sentence parsing, pre-trained models for document and clause types, and extractors for money, dates, constraints, courts, regulations, and citations. For lawyers building custom review or intake systems, it is one of the few repositories here that is explicitly legal-first rather than general-purpose AI infrastructure. GitHub shows **771 stars**, **Jupyter Notebook** as the primary language, an **AGPL-3.0** license, and a **last commit date of May 27, 2024**.
