+> **Description:** pyannote.audio’s README centers on speaker diarization, speech activity detection, overlapped speech detection, and speaker embeddings. In legal contexts, that is important because many workflows depend on “who spoke when,” not just the raw words. It pairs well with transcription engines and is particularly useful for hearings, multi-party meetings, interviews, and deposition recordings where speaker boundaries matter. GitHub shows **9,124 stars**, **Jupyter Notebook** as the primary language, an **MIT** license, and a **last commit date of February 7, 2026**.
